@node Differential Interactions
@section Interacting with a computation

Once a computation is written, interacting with it reduces to changing
the inputs and observing the output changes.  There is deliberate
latitude in how, which lets a program trade latency for throughput.

@subsection Creating inputs

A differential input is usually a @code{dtfl_input_session}: create one
with @code{dtfl_input_session_new} (over a record type and a diff
class), add it to a scope with
@code{dtfl_input_session_to_collection}, and the collection it returns
is the bridge from imperative code into the dataflow.  The scope's time
class must be totally ordered.

@subsection Making changes

Three calls change a collection.  @code{dtfl_input_session_insert} adds
one copy of a record (a diff of @code{+1}); @code{dtfl_input_session_remove}
retracts one (@code{-1}); both need an Abelian diff class.
@code{dtfl_input_session_update} adds an arbitrary diff, and
@code{dtfl_input_session_update_at} adds one at a future time.  These are
buffered at the session's current epoch until flushed.

@subsection Advancing time

@code{dtfl_input_session_advance_to} moves the session's epoch forward to
a time at or after the current one (it panics otherwise).  Advancing is
the promise that no more changes will be made at an earlier time, which
is what lets downstream operators finalize their output for the times
left behind.  Batch changes under one epoch when you can: progress
tracking does work per distinct time, so a new time for every single
change is expensive.

@subsection Observing probes

A probe reports the frontier at the point it is attached, exactly as in
timely.  @code{dtfl_collection_probe_with} attaches one to a collection;
@code{dtfl_probe_less_than(probe, dtfl_input_session_time(input))} is
true while the output has not yet caught up to the input's current time.

@subsection Performing work

All the computation happens in @code{dtfl_worker_step}: each call
schedules the operators and performs some outstanding work, and repeated
calls bring the output probes in line with the inputs they depend on.
When a worker function returns, libdtfl steps the worker until the
computation completes (the inputs close as their sessions are closed),
so a program that only loads changes and returns still runs them all to
completion.  Explicit stepping matters when a program keeps interactive
access to a probe and does not want to run straight to the end:

@example
dtfl_input_session_advance_to(input, &next);
dtfl_input_session_flush(input);
while (dtfl_probe_less_than(probe, dtfl_input_session_time(input)))
        dtfl_worker_step(w);
@end example
